'''barmkin''' (bärm′kin), ''n.'' [Also spelled ''barmkyn, barnekin, barnkyn''; < ME. ''barmeken, barnekynch''; origin uncertain; possibly < ''barm''<sup>3</sup> = ''berm'', brim, border, edge (the forms in ''barn-'' being then corruptions), + ''-kin''; but more prob. all corruptions of ''barbican''.] The rampart or outer fortification of a castle. [Lowland Scotch and North. Eng.]
{{smaller block|<poem>And broad and bloody rose the sun,
And on the ''barmkyn'' shone.</poem>
{{right|''Old ballad'', in Boucher’s Border Minstrelsy, ii. 341.}}
<poem>Lord Soulis he sat in Hermitage Castle,
And Redcap was not by;
And he called on a page, who was witty and sage,
To go to the ''barmkin'' high.</poem>
{{right|''J. Leyden'', Lord Soulis, in N. and Q., 6th ser., XI. 386.}}
Battlements and ''barmkins'' and all the other appurtenances of Strength, as such places were called. {{right|''Lever.''}}}}

'''barmote''' (bär′mōt), ''n.'' [A reduction of earlier ''bargemote'', also ''barghmote'' and ''berghmote'', < G. ''berg'', a hill, mine, + E. ''mote'', meeting. Cf. ''barmaster''.] A court established in the reign of Edward III. and held twice a year in Derbyshire, England, in which matters connected with mining are considered. Also written ''bergmote''.

'''barmy''' (bär′mi), ''a.'' [< ''barm''<sup>2</sup> + ''-y''<sup>1</sup>.] Containing or resembling barm or yeast; frothy.
{{smaller block|<poem>Of windy cider and of ''barmy'' beer.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', tr. of Virgil’s Georgics, iii.}}
<poem>Why, thou bottle-ale,
Thou ''barmie'' froth!</poem>}}
